From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dmitry Afonchenko <undermove@yandex.ru>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:24:35 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 116/297] Meridian: builds, runs and passes its unit tests on
 Windows

Meridian's own code was written against POSIX. What that meant on Windows,
and what each part does there now:

- Paths. base::FilePath holds wide strings on Windows, so every place that
  mixed it with std::string says which it means: AppendASCII/AppendUTF8,
  FromUTF8Unsafe, AsUTF8Unsafe. Paths a project hands to the Workbench's
  page or to agents are written with "/" on every system (SlashPath).

- The Workbench's file access. It relies on a few POSIX promises: an open
  that refuses a symlink, a file's identity taken from the handle, a name
  looked up inside a folder that is held open. win/posix_files.{h,cc} gives
  the same calls with the same promises over Win32, so the hardened code
  stays one piece. base::MakeAbsoluteFilePath doesn't resolve links on
  Windows; RealFilePath (real_path.h) does, everywhere.

- Sockets to the helper processes (hooks of the coding agents, the secret
  guard): unix-domain sockets on Windows too, through Winsock
  (local_socket.h, win/posix_sockets.h). The agent server, which sits on
  net's server sockets, listens on a loopback port there; the token in the
  profile folder is what lets a client in, as before.

- The helper programs themselves. --secret-guard, --mission-hook and
  --mcp-bridge were only looked for in ChromeMain on macOS, so on Linux
  and Windows the binary started a browser instead. One call does it on
  every system now (helper_modes.h); on Windows it takes the arguments
  from the command line and leaves stdin and stdout as they are.

- Processes. RunProcess() on Windows keeps the child in a job and ends the
  job where POSIX signals the process group (ai/process_runner_win.cc).
  Terminals are pseudoconsoles (workbench/terminal_win.cc). The shell is
  PowerShell or the command prompt, with no login mode.

- No sandbox, no run. sandbox-exec is macOS's; on Linux and Windows
  SandboxedArgv() returns no command, so a CLI agent asked about a page
  doesn't start instead of starting unconfined.

- File modes (0600/0700) go through MakePrivate(), which has nothing to do
  on Windows: what Meridian writes there is under the person's profile and
  temp folders.

- Its own data folder. Off macOS the profile went where Chromium keeps
  its own (~/.config/chromium, %LOCALAPPDATA%\Chromium), so the two would
  have shared one. Now ~/.config/meridian-browser and
  %LOCALAPPDATA%\Meridian Browser, and Windows lists the browser as
  Meridian among the default apps.

The unit tests run on Windows with the system's own shell; tests that need
a symlink to a file make one where the account may (test_links.h) and leave
that part out otherwise.

